\item{v_digits}{Defunct. Renamed \code{effect_size_digits} in spicy 0.14.0,
as in the other table functions; supplying \code{v_digits} is an error
(class \code{spicy_defunct}).}
}
\value{
Depends on \code{output}:
\itemize{
\item \code{"default"}: a \code{data.frame} of class
\code{"spicy_categorical_table"} carrying the rendering metadata as
attributes. Its columns are internal and can change without
notice: read the numbers from \code{"data.frame"}, \code{"long"},
\code{\link[broom:tidy]{broom::tidy()}}, \code{\link[broom:glance]{broom::glance()}} or \code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}}. The object
is returned visibly, so a bare \code{table_categorical(...)} call
auto-prints the styled ASCII table at the console while
\code{t <- table_categorical(...)} stays silent (print \code{t} to display
the table).
\item \code{"data.frame"}: the displayed table with its numbers, what
\link[=as.data.frame.spicy_categorical_table]{as.data.frame()} of the
default object returns: one row per printed row -- the header row
of each variable, which carries its \emph{p}-value and its association
measure, then one row per level. The key columns are \code{variable}
(the column name), \code{label} (the label the rows display for it,
see \code{labels}), \code{level} and \code{.row_role} (\code{"factor_header"},
\code{"level"}, \code{"missing"} for the row of missing values the table
adds, or \code{"total"} for the Total row of \code{total_row = TRUE}), then
the printed columns: one pair \verb{<group> n} /
\verb{<group> \%} per group level (plus \code{Total} when
\code{include_total = TRUE}), \code{p} (absent with \code{p_value = FALSE}), the
association measure (named by its header: the symbol of the
measure, \code{V}, its name for a measure without one, or \verb{Effect size}
when the row variables do not share one), its bounds \verb{95\% CI LL} /
\verb{95\% CI UL} with \code{assoc_ci = TRUE}, and \code{SMD} with \code{smd = TRUE}.
When \code{by = NULL}, the printed columns are \code{n} and \verb{\%}.
\item \code{"long"}: a long \code{data.frame}, one row per variable, level and
group, with columns \code{variable}, \code{label}, \code{level}, \code{.row_role} (the
role of \code{level}: \code{"level"}, \code{"missing"} for the row of missing
values, or \code{"total"} for the Total row of \code{total_row = TRUE}), then,
when \code{by} is used, \code{group}, \code{.group_role} (the role
of \code{group}: \code{"group"}, \code{"missing"} for the column of missing \code{by}
values, or \code{"total"} for the margin) and \code{total} (\code{TRUE} on the
rows of the margin and of the Total row), then \code{n}, \code{pct} (the
percentage the table
displays, in the direction of \code{percent}),
then, with \code{by}, the test of the variable unless
\code{p_value = FALSE}: \code{test_type} (\code{"chi_squared"} for Pearson's,
\code{"n1_chi_squared"} for the N-1 chi-squared, \code{"fisher_exact"},
\code{"fisher_midp"} for the Fisher mid-p test, or \code{"fisher_exact_mc"}
for the Monte Carlo estimate of Fisher's exact test), \code{statistic}
and \code{df} (the chi-squared, Pearson's or the N-1, \code{NA} for a
variable tested by an exact test), \code{p.value} (and \code{p_unadjusted}
under \code{p_adjust}); and the association measure, whichever measure
it is: \code{es_type} names it per row (\code{"cramer_v"}, \code{"phi"}, ...),
\code{es_value} holds it, \code{es_ci_lower} / \code{es_ci_upper} its interval
(\code{NA} unless \code{assoc_ci = TRUE}), and \code{smd_type} (\code{"binary"} or
\code{"multinomial"}, the kernel the value came from) / \code{smd_value} the
standardized mean difference. The measure and SMD columns are
there whether or not they were requested, \code{NA} when not, so the
schema does not move with an argument. The statistics of a
variable sit on every row of its block.
\item \code{"tinytable"}: a \code{tinytable} object.
\item \code{"gt"}: a \code{gt_tbl} object.
\item \code{"flextable"}: a \code{flextable} object.
\item \code{"excel"} / \code{"word"}: writes to disk and returns the file
path invisibly.
\item \code{"clipboard"}: copies the table and returns the copied text
invisibly.
}

The note opens with the sample size, "N = 159." or, with \code{by},
"N = 159: Women (n = 80), Men (n = 79).": the rows of the data the table
describes, and those of each group column in column order, the column
of missing \code{by} values included, so that the n add up to N. A row
missing \code{by} under \code{drop_na = TRUE} and a row with a missing weight
leave the table and are not counted; the note says how many. Under
\code{weights} the cells hold weighted counts and N counts rows: "N = 60
(unweighted).". One N per table, the missing values of a variable in
its \code{"(Missing)"} row or in the note. With \code{by}, the direction of the
percentages follows, then the weights. Their base takes no sentence:
the table shows it (see \code{percent_base}).

The note, the \code{drop_na = TRUE} disclosure included, travels with the
table on every route, not just the console: \code{"default"} prints it
under the ASCII table, \code{"tinytable"} / \code{"gt"} / \code{"flextable"} /
\code{"word"} carry it as a table note, \code{"excel"} writes it below the body,
and the default object and the data frames of \code{"data.frame"} /
\code{"long"} keep the whole note as printed, its lead-in included, in
\code{attr(x, "note")} (\code{NULL} when there is nothing to say), so a pipeline
that renders the numbers itself can still state what left the table.
The default object also keeps the title the console prints, in
\code{attr(x, "title")}.
The same three objects record the direction of their percentages in
\code{attr(x, "percent")}: \code{"column"} or \code{"row"} (see \code{percent}), and
\code{"column"} for a table without \code{by}; and their base in
\code{attr(x, "percent_base")}, \code{"valid"} or \code{"total"}. The percentage
columns of \code{"data.frame"}, \code{pct} in \code{"long"} and the \code{proportion} of
\code{\link[broom:tidy]{broom::tidy()}} hold the shares the table displays, in that direction
and on that base: \code{NA} on the \code{"(Missing)"} row under \code{"valid"}.
On the \code{"tinytable"} route the note is set one size down;
\code{options(spicy.note_style)} governs that (see \code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}}).

The Excel sheet carries the same title the console prints on its
first row; the table itself starts on row 3.
}
\description{
Builds a publication-ready frequency or cross-tabulation table for one
or many categorical variables selected with tidyselect syntax.

With \code{by}, produces grouped cross-tabulation summaries (using
\code{\link[=cross_tab]{cross_tab()}} internally) with a \emph{p}-value per variable, from Pearson's
chi-squared test or Fisher's exact test (see \code{test}), and optional
association measures.
Without \code{by}, produces one-way frequency-style summaries.

Multiple output formats are available via \code{output}: a printed ASCII
table (\code{"default"}), a numeric \code{data.frame} (\code{"data.frame"}, the
displayed table, or \code{"long"}, one row per variable, level and group),
or publication-ready tables (\code{"tinytable"}, \code{"gt"}, \code{"flextable"},
\code{"excel"}, \code{"clipboard"}, \code{"word"}).
}
\section{Tests}{
When \code{by} is used, each selected variable is cross-tabulated
against the grouping variable with \code{\link[=cross_tab]{cross_tab()}} and tested on the
complete cases of its observed levels, a \code{"(Missing)"} level and a
declared level nobody holds left out: one \emph{p}-value per variable,
in the \code{p} column. A table left with a single row or column (a
variable observed at one level, or in one group) is uninformative and
gets no test and no association measure, whatever \code{test} and
\code{assoc_measure} say: its \code{p} and measure cells show an en dash, the
undefined cell, and the note names no measure for it. A blank cell
only means a statistic that was not requested or does not apply.

The chi-squared approximation is unreliable when expected counts are
small. Under \code{test = "auto"}, the default, a variable takes Pearson's
chi-squared test (\code{\link[stats:chisq.test]{stats::chisq.test()}}) when every expected count of
its table is at least 5, and Fisher's exact test
(\code{\link[stats:fisher.test]{stats::fisher.test()}}) otherwise: the rule gtsummary's \code{add_p()}
applies, with the same p-values. Fisher's exact test of a table
larger than 2x2 is the Freeman-Halton extension. When its exact
enumeration fails, which happens on large sparse tables, the
p-value is its Monte Carlo estimate over \code{simulate_B} tables drawn
with the observed margins: it is random, so call \code{\link[=set.seed]{set.seed()}} first
to reproduce it. \code{test = "chisq"} tests every variable by the
chi-squared, the behavior before 0.14.0, and \code{test = "fisher"} every
variable by the exact test.

Fisher's exact test is conservative: its actual level is often well
below the nominal one, at a cost in power. \code{test = "midp"} gives a 2x2
table (a two-level variable by two groups) the Fisher mid-p test, as
Fagerland, Lydersen and Laake (2017, equation 1.12, p. 28, and section
4.4.6, p. 97) and Lydersen, Fagerland and Laake (2009, equation 12,
p. 1165) define it: the tables with the observed margins are ordered
by their hypergeometric probability, and the \emph{p}-value adds the
probabilities of the tables less probable than the observed one to
half the probabilities of the tables as probable, the observed one
included, ties being read within the relative tolerance of
\code{\link[stats:fisher.test]{stats::fisher.test()}}. The mid-p test can exceed the nominal level,
usually by little, and Fagerland, Lydersen and Laake (2017, p. 175)
recommend it for all sample sizes. A variable whose table is not 2x2
follows \code{"auto"}, and the note names the test of each variable. The
central variant, twice the smaller one-sided mid-p (the \code{exact2x2} and
\code{epitools} packages; Agresti, 2019, p. 49), coincides with this one
when the row totals or the column totals are equal, except that it
gives 1 for the most probable table; it differs otherwise and is not
computed.

\code{test = "n1"} applies the rule of Campbell (2007, pp. 18-19) to a 2x2
table. When every expected count is at least 1, the table takes the
N-1 chi-squared test: Pearson's statistic, with no continuity
correction, multiplied by (N - 1)/N and referred to a chi-squared
distribution with 1 degree of freedom, which is the Mantel-Haenszel
statistic of a single 2x2 table; \code{statistic} and \code{df} hold that
statistic in the long output.
Otherwise it takes Fisher's exact test, two-sided by Irwin's rule as
\code{\link[stats:fisher.test]{stats::fisher.test()}} computes it. Campbell found the N-1 test close
to its nominal level under that restriction, not without it. A
variable whose table is not 2x2 follows \code{"auto"}, and the note names
the test of each variable.

The note names the test behind the \emph{p}-values, "P-values: Pearson
chi-squared test (sex, education); Fisher exact test (smoking).", or
the one test when it covers them all, "P-value:" when the table shows
a single \emph{p}-value. \code{test_type} in the long output gives the test of
each variable. See \verb{@param correct} /
\code{simulate_p} to switch on Yates' continuity correction or Monte Carlo
\emph{p}-values for the chi-squared test, and \verb{@param assoc_measure} for
the per-row dispatch table used by \code{"auto"} (2x2 -> Phi, both ordered
-> Kendall's Tau-b, otherwise Cramér's V). Without \code{by}, the table
reports the marginal frequency distribution of each variable with no
inferential statistics.

The exact tests condition on counts. Under \code{weights}, they run on the
weighted table when its cells are whole numbers (frequency weights);
otherwise a variable that a rule sends to Fisher's exact test keeps
the chi-squared test, Pearson's under \code{test = "auto"} and the N-1
under Campbell's rule, with a warning (\code{spicy_fallback}), and
\code{test = "fisher"}, or \code{test = "midp"} on a 2x2 table, is an error. For
survey weights, \code{\link[=table_categorical_svy]{table_categorical_svy()}} gives the design-based test.

The test and the measure are two decisions with two arguments:
\code{p_value = FALSE} removes the test, \code{assoc_measure = "none"} the
measure. Together they give a purely descriptive table, which keeps
the group columns and their headers; \code{smd} stays available.

For model-based comparisons (cluster-robust SE, weighted contrasts,
fitted means) on continuous outcomes, see \code{\link[=table_continuous_lm]{table_continuous_lm()}}.
For descriptive (empirical) comparisons on continuous outcomes, see
\code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}}.
}

\section{Groups with no data}{
A variable with no observed value in a group of \code{by} that holds rows,
typically a question not asked in that group, has no data there, which
is not a missing answer. Its cells in that group show an en dash
(\code{NA} in the data frames, in \link[=tidy.spicy_categorical_table]{tidy()}
and in \code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}}), and its rows there are left out of
everything computed for that variable: the missing values the note
counts, the \code{Total} column, the \code{"(Missing)"} row, the test, the
association measure and the SMD. A variable left with fewer than two
groups with data is not compared: its \code{p} and measure cells show the
dash, and the note names no test and no measure for it. The note
says "No data for X in G.", in one sentence for the variables with no
data in the same groups ("No data for X and Y in G."). Without \code{by}, a
variable with no observed value at all keeps its declared levels (its
one row when it declares none) with the dash, and the note says "No
data for X." The column of missing \code{by} values is never such a group,
and a declared level of \code{by} that holds no row keeps its zero column.
}

\section{Multiple-comparison adjustment}{
The p-values of a descriptive table describe: they test no
prespecified hypothesis. STROBE (explanation of item 14) and the
SAMPL guidelines advise against significance tests in descriptive and
baseline tables, hence the default \code{p_adjust = "none"}.

When a journal or a protocol asks for adjusted p-values anyway,
\code{p_adjust} applies \code{\link[stats:p.adjust]{stats::p.adjust()}} to the family of every test in
the table: one p-value per variable (one per block in
\code{\link[=table_outcome]{table_outcome()}}), never the rows of one variable, which share one
test. \code{"holm"} suits a confirmatory set of comparisons, \code{"BH"} a
screening one; \code{\link[=table_regression]{table_regression()}} lists the methods. A test that
returned no p-value stays out of the family.

The adjustment runs before any formatting, so the \code{p} column and its
\verb{<.001} threshold show the adjusted values, and the table note gives
the number of tests and the method. The raw p-values stay in the
object: a \code{p_unadjusted} column next to the p-value column of the
returned data frames, and \code{col_meta$p$p_unadjusted} (with the method
in \code{p_adjust} and the family size in \code{p_adjust_m}) in
\code{\link[=as_structured]{as_structured()}}. Without \code{by}, or with \code{p_value = FALSE}, the table
shows no p-value to adjust, and a \code{p_adjust} other than \code{"none"} is an
error.
}

\section{Standardized mean difference}{
\code{smd = TRUE} adds an \code{SMD} column with the balance diagnostic of
the Table 1 literature, on the variable row beside \code{p}. For a
two-category variable it is the Bernoulli form,

\deqn{\mathrm{SMD} = \frac{p_1 - p_2}{\sqrt{(p_1(1-p_1) +
p_2(1-p_2)) / 2}}}

with \eqn{p} the proportion of the SECOND category, \strong{signed},
group 1 minus group 2 in the order the table displays them. Note
the denominator: the Bernoulli variance \eqn{p(1-p)} at \emph{n}, not
\code{var()} at \eqn{n-1}, which would be 19\% off on a small table.

"Second category" is the order the table shows, which is worth
knowing for a \strong{logical} variable: spicy displays \code{TRUE} then
\code{FALSE}, so the sign is taken on \code{FALSE}, where \code{tableone} and
\code{cobalt} coerce with \code{factor()} (\code{FALSE}, \code{TRUE}) and take it on
\code{TRUE} -- the same magnitude with the opposite sign. Convert to a
factor with the level order you want if the direction matters.

For three or more categories it is the multivariate form of Yang
and Dalton (2012, SAS Global Forum 335-2012),

\deqn{\mathrm{SMD} = \sqrt{T' S^{-} T}}

with \eqn{T} the difference of the two profiles of proportions
(first category dropped) and \eqn{S} the mean of their multinomial
covariance matrices. This is a Mahalanobis distance: it is
\strong{unsigned}, it is \strong{not bounded by 1}, and \eqn{S^{-}} is a
pseudo-inverse, because a declared-but-unobserved category makes
\eqn{S} singular and \code{solve()} would abort where the
pseudo-inverse returns exactly the value that category's absence
implies. Which kernel a row took is published as \code{smd_type} in the
\code{"long"} output, and the unsigned reading is stated in the table
note whenever a variable has more than two categories. The
\code{MASS} package is needed for this arm only.

Two profiles with \strong{no category in common} have an infinite
standardized distance. The pseudo-inverse would quietly publish a
finite number there, so the cell is an en-dash and a classed
warning says why. The same applies when each group is constant on
a different category, where the naive route publishes \code{0} --
"perfectly balanced" for the most imbalanced variable possible.

Conventions shared with \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}}: exactly two groups
(three or more are refused, not averaged over pairs); complete
cases on the observed groups, so a \code{drop_na = FALSE} "(Missing)"
level is displayed and never enters the diagnostic; no confidence
interval and no p-value, by design. Under \code{weights} the profiles
are the weighted proportions, which makes this column agree with
both the frequency and the survey-design readings -- a profile of
proportions is invariant to a global rescaling of the weights, so
\code{rescale} cannot move it. (Only the continuous arm has a
convention to choose there.)

The \code{SMD} cell keeps its leading zero where the association cell
drops it: the APA strip belongs to a bounded measure, and this one
is not bounded. The two columns therefore print \code{0.45} and \code{.45}
side by side, on purpose.

A balance table usually shows no test: \code{p_value = FALSE} removes the
\code{p} column here as it does in \code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}}, so a table mixing
continuous and categorical variables can drop both. \code{\link[=inline]{inline()}}
quotes the cell from the variable row (\code{inline(tbl, smoking, column = "smd")}).
}

\section{Display conventions}{
Decimal alignment, \emph{p}-value formatting, and required suggested
packages per output engine are documented under \verb{@param align},
\verb{@param p_digits}, and \verb{@param output} respectively.

Counts are displayed as integers: weighted counts are rounded
(ties half to even, the R convention) at display time only, in
cells and margins alike -- the SPSS Crosstabs convention. Cells
and margins are rounded independently, so small display
discrepancies are possible (e.g. two cells of exactly 0.5 each
display as \code{0} while their \code{Total} of 1.0 displays as \code{1}). The
machine outputs (\code{"data.frame"}, \code{"long"}) carry the exact
weighted counts and full-precision percentages.
}

\section{Declared missing values}{


Survey files imported with \strong{haven} often carry \emph{declared missing
values}: codes such as \verb{8 = Don't know} or \code{9 = Refused} that the
source file marks as missing while keeping them distinct from a
plain \code{NA}. Two kinds of declaration exist: \code{na_values} / \code{na_range}
metadata on \code{\link[haven:labelled_spss]{haven::labelled_spss()}} vectors, and tagged missing
values created by \code{\link[haven:tagged_na]{haven::tagged_na()}} (the Stata \code{.a}, \code{.b}, ...
convention). \code{\link[haven:read_sav]{haven::read_sav()}} keeps the \code{na_values} / \code{na_range}
declaration only with \code{user_na = TRUE}: its default turns the
declared codes into \code{NA} on import.

spicy honors the declaration by default (\code{user_na = TRUE}):
declared missing values are excluded from every statistic exactly
like \code{NA} -- valid percentages, means, chi-squared tests,
association measures, row-wise summaries, and group definitions --
but they are not erased from display. \code{\link[=freq]{freq()}} lists each observed
declared value as its own row of the Missing block, with its value
label; \code{\link[=cross_tab]{cross_tab()}}, \code{\link[=table_categorical]{table_categorical()}}, and
\code{\link[=table_continuous]{table_continuous()}} disclose the exclusion in the table note
(\verb{Declared missing values removed: x (2).}); \code{\link[=varlist]{varlist()}} counts
them as missing in \code{N_valid} / \code{NAs} / \code{N_distinct} while still
listing the declared codes in \code{Values}; \code{\link[=code_book]{code_book()}} counts them in
\code{n_declared_missing} and flags them in its \code{values} table.

Every function involved offers the same escape hatch: set
\code{user_na = FALSE} to ignore the declaration and treat the declared
codes as valid values (the behavior of spicy before 0.13.0).
Tagged missing values are genuine \code{NA}s either way; for them,
\code{user_na = FALSE} only collapses the per-tag breakdown back into
the regular \code{NA} count.
}

\examples{
# --- Basic usage ---------------------------------------------------------

# Default: ASCII console table grouped by sex.
table_categorical(
  sochealth,
  select = c(smoking, physical_activity),
  by = sex
)

# One-way frequency-style table (no `by`).
table_categorical(
  sochealth,
  select = c(smoking, physical_activity)
)

# Purely descriptive grouped table: no test, no association measure.
table_categorical(
  sochealth,
  select = c(smoking, physical_activity),
  by = sex,
  p_value = FALSE,
  assoc_measure = "none"
)

# A small sample: `smoking` has an expected count below 5 and takes
# Fisher's exact test; the note names the test of each variable.
table_categorical(
  sochealth[1:40, ],
  select = c(smoking, physical_activity),
  by = sex
)

# Pretty labels keyed by column name.
table_categorical(
  sochealth,
  select = c(smoking, physical_activity),
  by = education,
  labels = c(
    smoking           = "Current smoker",
    physical_activity = "Physical activity"
  )
)

# Survey weights with rescaling.
table_categorical(
  sochealth,
  select = c(smoking, physical_activity),
  by = education,
  weights = "weight",
  rescale = TRUE
)

# Confidence interval for the association measure.
table_categorical(
  sochealth,
  select = smoking,
  by = education,
  assoc_ci = TRUE
)

# --- Per-variable association measure ----------------------------------

# Default (`assoc_measure = "auto"`): one measure per row variable based on
# the variable type (2x2 -> Phi, both ordered factors -> Kendall's Tau-b,
# otherwise Cramer's V). When the chosen measures differ across rows, the
# column header collapses to `"Effect size"` and the note names the
# measure used for each variable.
table_categorical(
  sochealth,
  select = c(smoking, education),
  by = sex
)

# Force a uniform measure across all row variables: its symbol, V,
# heads the column, and the note defines it.
table_categorical(
  sochealth,
  select = c(smoking, education),
  by = sex,
  assoc_measure = "cramer_v"
)

# Per-variable override (recommended named form).
table_categorical(
  sochealth,
  select = c(smoking, education, self_rated_health),
  by = sex,
  assoc_measure = c(
    smoking           = "phi",        # binary x binary
    education         = "cramer_v",   # multi-category nominal
    self_rated_health = "tau_b"       # ordinal x binary, Tau-b
  )
)

# --- Output formats -----------------------------------------------------

# The rendered outputs below all wrap the same call:
#   table_categorical(sochealth,
#                     select = c(smoking, physical_activity),
#                     by = sex)
# only `output` changes. Assign each result to a variable -- some
# engines auto-print as a console-friendly text fallback inside
# the `?` help viewer.

# Wide data.frame (one row per modality).
table_categorical(
  sochealth,
  select = c(smoking, physical_activity),
  by = sex,
  output = "data.frame"
)

# Long data.frame (one row per (modality x group)).
table_categorical(
  sochealth,
  select = c(smoking, physical_activity),
  by = sex,
  output = "long"
)

\donttest{
# Rendered HTML / docx objects -- best viewed inside a
# Quarto / R Markdown document or a pkgdown article.
if (requireNamespace("tinytable", quietly = TRUE)) {
  tt <- table_categorical(
    sochealth, select = c(smoking, physical_activity), by = sex,
    output = "tinytable"
  )
}
if (requireNamespace("gt", quietly = TRUE)) {
  tbl <- table_categorical(
    sochealth, select = c(smoking, physical_activity), by = sex,
    output = "gt"
  )
}
if (requireNamespace("flextable", quietly = TRUE)) {
  ft <- table_categorical(
    sochealth, select = c(smoking, physical_activity), by = sex,
    output = "flextable"
  )
}

# Excel and Word: write to a temporary file.
if (requireNamespace("openxlsx2", quietly = TRUE)) {
  tmp <- tempfile(fileext = ".xlsx")
  table_categorical(
    sochealth, select = c(smoking, physical_activity), by = sex,
    output = "excel", excel_path = tmp
  )
  unlink(tmp)
}
if (
  requireNamespace("flextable", quietly = TRUE) &&
    requireNamespace("officer", quietly = TRUE)
) {
  tmp <- tempfile(fileext = ".docx")
  table_categorical(
    sochealth, select = c(smoking, physical_activity), by = sex,
    output = "word", word_path = tmp
  )
  unlink(tmp)
}
}

\dontrun{
# Clipboard: writes to the system clipboard.
